steps.body:
An `else` block runs when the `if` condition is false. Exactly one of the two blocks runs, never both and never neither.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	n := 7
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
if n%2 == 0 {
	fmt.Println("even")
} else {
	fmt.Println("odd")
}
```

Keep `} else {` on one line. Go adds a semicolon at the end of any line that ends in `}`, so an `else` on the next line is a separate statement and fails to compile with `syntax error: unexpected else, expecting }`. gofmt always writes it the joined way.

Each block is its own scope: a variable made with `:=` inside a branch is gone after the closing brace. A variable from an `if` init statement is the exception you can lean on: it is visible in the `else` too.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	n := 7
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
if r := n % 3; r == 0 {
	fmt.Println("divisible")
} else {
	fmt.Println("remainder", r)
}
```

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	n := 7
	if n%2 == 0 {
		fmt.Println(n, "is even")
	} else {
		fmt.Println(n, "is odd")
	}

	score := 72
	if score >= 60 {
		fmt.Println("Pass")
	} else {
		fmt.Println("Fail")
	}
}

