steps.body:
Any block can hold another `if`, so you can nest a decision inside an `if` or an `else`. Nesting fits rules that refine each other, like leap years: divisible by 4, except that a century must also be divisible by 400.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	year := 1900
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(year, "divisible by 4:", year%4 == 0, "century:", year%100 == 0)
}
```

```go
if year%4 == 0 {
	if year%100 == 0 {
		// a century: check 400
	} else {
		// a leap year
	}
}
```

Deep nesting is hard to read, so you can often fold it into one condition. An `if a { if b { ... } }` with no `else` is the same as `if a && b { ... }`. When either of two paths leads to the same result, join them with `||`. The whole leap year rule becomes:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	year := 2024
	leap :=
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(year, "is a leap year:", leap)
}
```

```go
year%4 == 0 && (year%100 != 0 || year%400 == 0)
```

Keep the parentheses. `&&` binds tighter than `||`, so without them Go reads `(year%4 == 0 && year%100 != 0) || year%400 == 0`, which here happens to agree but often does not. Parentheses make the grouping you mean explicit.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	year := 1900
	if year%4 == 0 {
		if year%100 == 0 {
			if year%400 == 0 {
				fmt.Println(year, "is a leap year")
			} else {
				fmt.Println(year, "is not a leap year")
			}
		} else {
			fmt.Println(year, "is a leap year")
		}
	} else {
		fmt.Println(year, "is not a leap year")
	}

	year = 2024
	if year%4 == 0 && (year%100 != 0 || year%400 == 0) {
		fmt.Println(year, "is a leap year")
	} else {
		fmt.Println(year, "is not a leap year")
	}
}

